initial.prompt:
Use case: stylized-concept, reference-led game asset extraction/reconstruction.
Asset type: transparent factory frame and lever-station PARTS sprite atlas for Tilt Shift.
Input image 1 is the user's exact mockup, visual source. Extract/reconstruct the warm cardboard/wood toy side framing and small operator consoles without any characters.
One landscape 2048x1536 PNG with genuine alpha transparency, exactly EIGHT separate objects in a strict four-column by two-row grid, equally spaced with wide transparent gutters and 40px minimum outer padding.
Top row, left to right:
1 the tall LEFT factory side rail from the mockup, warm tan corrugated/cardboard crafted column with brown inner edge and a few round metal rivets, no console or character attached;
2 matching tall RIGHT side rail with inner edge on opposite side, same dimensions and styling;
3 one compact warm tan operator-console/platform body from under the reference characters, a front-facing wall-mountable unit with tiny corner fasteners, dark gray lever socket area on top, no character, no lever, no label attached;
4 one blank pale rectangular player-name plaque with soft rounded paper/card edges, no letters or numerals.
Bottom row:
5 one slim light tan lever SHAFT only, straight vertically upright, no knob, with clean rounded lower pivot end;
6 one orange spherical lever grip/knob;
7 one matching blue spherical lever grip/knob, exactly same shape and size;
8 one separate round dark gray lever pivot socket.
Station body is neutral so identical instances can support five operators on each side. Shaft, knobs and pivot are separate for future rotation and hand alignment; no moving parts baked onto the stationary body. Keep a simple front/arena viewpoint consistent with the source, shallow visible top surfaces only, soft upper-left material lighting, tactile bevels and subtly paper-textured toy construction. No independent shadows on canvas, background, characters, hands, feet, balls, paddles, baskets, captions, text, drawn checkerboard, cell guides or extra objects. Objects do not touch or overlap and are not cropped. Faithful source art, reusable construction parts rather than an assembled gameplay scene.
